<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Copyright>Copyright © 2015-$([System.DateTime]::Now.Year) Akka.NET Team</Copyright>
    <Authors>Akka.NET Team</Authors>
    <Tags>akka, persistence, durable entities, workflows</Tags>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
    <LangVersion>preview</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <VersionPrefix>0.1.0-alpha.11</VersionPrefix>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>**Headers beside a command.** A few opaque strings travel beside a command, so an application can say which request a chain of work belongs to without putting that into its command and event types. Akka.Durable is still a proof of concept, not an official Akka.NET project.

* **`IHeaderPropagator`.** `Inject` adds headers at every send. The library carries them, stores them with each recorded effect, and calls `Restore` before an effect handler runs, also for a retry and after a restart. `InjectForTimer` lets an application mark a chain that one of an entity's own timers started. Register one with `AddDurableHeaderPropagator&lt;T&gt;()`. `Decide`, `Evolve` and effect handlers never see headers.
* **`Headers` on the observations.** `EventSaved`, `EffectObserved` and `TimerObserved` have a new `Headers` member.
* **Fix: a timer whose command `Decide` stashed was reported as `Fired` twice.** It is now reported once, when its command is decided.
* **New guide page,** "Headers beside a command".

**What you change from 0.1.0-alpha.10:** three things.

1. Code that constructs `EventSaved`, `EffectObserved` or `TimerObserved` itself (tests, usually) passes the new `Headers` member. Observers that only read them need no change.
2. Roll out in two steps if you use headers in a cluster. First run this version on every node. Only then register a propagator, because a node on an older version cannot read a command that carries headers.
3. Nothing to migrate in storage. Each recorded effect gains one new field. Records written before it still read, and an older version skips the field.</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Target framework definitions. Projects should reference these instead of hardcoding a TFM:
         libraries use $(NetLibVersion), test projects use $(NetTestVersion).
         NetStandardLibVersion is retained for Akka.NET parity and downstream repos that reference it. -->
    <NetStandardLibVersion>net11.0</NetStandardLibVersion>
    <NetLibVersion>net11.0</NetLibVersion>
    <NetTestVersion>net11.0</NetTestVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!-- GitHub SourceLink -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub" PrivateAssets="All" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- NuGet package properties -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)\akkalogo.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!-- NuGet .nupkg options -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageTags>akka;actors;actor model;Akka;concurrency;test</PackageTags>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Aaronontheweb/Akka.Durable</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageIcon>akkalogo.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <!-- Optional: Embed source files that are not tracked by the source control manager in the PDB -->
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <!-- Optional: Build symbol package (.snupkg) to distribute the PDB containing Source Link -->
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>